d as substitutions in order, from top to bottom, and each later rule acts on the colours already produced by the earlier ones.\n\n- **Pair 1** has the legend 1→4, 3→5, 4→6, 6→7.\n  - A canvas 1 goes 1→4, then 4→6, then 6→7, so it ends as **7**.\n  - A canvas 6 becomes **7**, and a canvas 3 becomes **5**.\n  - This matches the output exactly: 1→7, 6→7, 3→5.\n- **Pair 2** has the order 1→4, 6→7, 3→5, 4→6.\n  - A 1 goes 1→4 and then 4→6, ending as **6**. The rule 6→7 had already been applied before the 1 turned into a 6, so it does not fire again.\n  - The 6 becomes 7 and the 3 becomes 5. This matches the output.\n- **Pair 3** has the legend 1→4, 6→7, 3→5, giving 1→**4**, 6→7, 3→5. This matches too.\n\nSo the rule is: compose the legend's substitutions in reading order into one colour map, then recolour every non-legend cell with it.\n\n**Finding the legend in general.** The test inputs move the legend elsewhere:\n- In test 2 it sits at the top right, above a horizontal divider.\n- In test 1 there is no divider at all. The legend is a 2×7 block at x=9..10, y=18..24, sitting inside the big 3-coloured outline.\n\nIn every grid the legend is a background-isolated, multicoloured component whose bounding box is completely filled and exactly 2 wide. No other object in these grids is like that: the shapes in test 1 are single-coloured and not filled rectangles. So I locate it with `objects(..., multicolour=True)`.\n\nThe divider and the other shapes are simply recoloured by the map wherever their colour appears as a source. For example, the 9-shape in test 1 has colour 9, which the legend sends to 2. Colours that are never a source stay unchanged.\n\n**Applying it to the tests.**\n- **Test 1**, legend top to bottom: 7→9, 2→3, 4→2, 6→7, 2→9, 7→8, 9→2. Composing these gives:\n  - 7 → 9 → 2 (the rule 7→8 no longer applies, because the cell is 9 by then)\n  - 6 → 7 → 8\n  - 4 → 2 → 9\n  - 9 → 2\n  - 2 → 3\n  - 3, 5 and 8 are never sources, so they stay unchanged.\n- **Test 2**, legend top to bottom: 1→2, 5→3, 6→7, 9→1, 1→3, 5→1, 2→4. Composing these gives:\n  - 1 → 2 → 4\n  - 5 → 3\n  - 6 → 7\n  - 9 → 1 → 3\n  - 2 → 4\n  - 3 and 8 stay unchanged, and so does the divider colour 4.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits one canvas per legend rule, applied in order to the non-legend cells, and then the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    legend = None\n    for o in objects(g, bg=bg, multicolour=True):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        h, w = y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1\n        if w == 2 and h >= 1 and o[\"size\"] == h * w:\n            block = g[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1]\n            if len(set(block.ravel().tolist())) > 1:\n                legend = o\n                break\n    if legend is None:\n        return g\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = legend[\"box\"]\n    mask = np.ones(g.shape, bool)\n    mask[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = False\n    out = g.copy()\n    for y in range(y0, y1 + 1):\n        a, b = int(g[y, x0]), int(g[y, x0 + 1])\n        sel = mask & (out == a)\n        if sel.any():\n            out[sel] = b\n            emit(out, f\"Apply legend rule {a} -> {b} to all non-legend cells currently coloured {a}.\")\n    emit(out, \"All legend substitutions applied in top-to-bottom order.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is fenced off from a \"canvas\" side by a grey-red divider, and the canvas holds a few scattered single pixels."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- In test 2 it sits at the top right, above a horizontal divider."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The divider and the other shapes are simply recoloured by the map wherever their colour appears as a source."}],"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Reading the legend.** Each legend row is a pair `a b`, meaning \"recolour a to b\"."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 1** has the legend 1→4, 3→5, 4→6, 6→7."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 3** has the legend 1→4, 6→7, 3→5, giving 1→**4**, 6→7, 3→5."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Reading the legend.** Each legend row is a pair `a b`, meaning \"recolour a to b\"."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The rows are applied as substitutions in order, from top to bottom, and each later rule acts on the colours already produced by the earlier ones."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is: compose the legend's substitutions in reading order into one colour map, then recolour every non-legend cell with it."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Finding the legend in general.** The test inputs move the legend elsewhere:"}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The legend is a 2×7 block at x=9..10, y=18..24, sitting inside the big 3-coloured outline."}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is: compose the legend's substitutions in reading order into one colour map, then recolour every non-legend cell with it."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"All legend substitutions applied in top-to-bottom order."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In every grid the legend is a background-isolated, multicoloured component whose bounding box is completely filled and exactly 2 wide."}],"instructions":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The rows are applied as substitutions in order, from top to bottom, and each later rule acts on the colours already produced by the earlier ones."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"90d94e3b665283b674b5690369766a83d1f08f825586d19a20ce7483d3d45133","task_sha256":"722c2fef358baa828702c1d2fc8767e46f7869727782bd5b8b5f68c8f752e6ae","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
